.TH CIRCUIT 2
.SH NAME
circuit: parse, dc, begin, beginfromdc, step, nodev, parti, solve \- lumped electrical circuits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "circuit.m";
circuit := load Circuit Circuit->PATH;
Net, Part: import circuit;

circuit->init();
(n, err) := circuit->parse(netlist);
err = circuit->dc(n);
err = circuit->begin(n);
err = circuit->beginfromdc(n);
err = circuit->step(n, h);
v := circuit->nodev(n, "out");
i := circuit->parti(n, "R1");
(x, err) = circuit->solve(a, b);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Circuit
analyses a lumped linear circuit given as a netlist, in the form every
engineering student is already taught.
.PP
.B Parse
reads a netlist and returns the circuit, or nil and a message saying
what was wrong with the text and on which line.
One component to a line:
.IP
.EX
R1 in out 1k
C1 out 0 1u
V1 in 0 5
\&.tran 10u 5m
.EE
.PP
The first letter of a component's name says what it is:
.BR R esistor,
.BR C apacitor,
.BR I nductor
(spelled
.BR L ),
.BR V oltage
source,
.BR I
current source.
Node
.B 0
(or
.BR gnd )
is ground; every other name is a node, created by being mentioned.
Values take SPICE's multipliers \-
.BR t ,
.BR g ,
.BR meg ,
.BR k ,
.BR m ,
.BR u ,
.BR n ,
.BR p ,
.B f
\- with
.B meg
and not
.B M
for a million, since
.B M
is milli.
A unit may follow, as in
.B 1kohm
or
.BR 10uF ,
and is ignored; anything else is refused rather than silently read as a
bare number.
.PP
Lines beginning with a dot are directives.
.B .dc
and
.B .tran
.I step
.I stop
say which analysis the netlist is for and are recorded in the
.B Net
for a caller to use;
.B .method
selects
.B trap
(the default) or
.BR be ;
and
.B .ic
.I part
.I value
gives a capacitor an initial voltage or an inductor an initial current.
.PP
.B Dc
solves the operating point: capacitors open, inductors shorted, which is
where the circuit ends up.
.PP
.B Begin
and
.B beginfromdc
set the initial condition for a transient, and one of them must be
called before the first
.BR step .
.B Begin
is the circuit at rest \- no charge on any capacitor and no current in
any inductor, except where a
.B .ic
line says otherwise.
.B Beginfromdc
is the circuit that has been switched on a long time.
Both then solve once more with every storage element holding the state
it has, to find the other half of that state: the current through each
capacitor and the voltage across each inductor.
At rest does not make those zero \- at
.I t
= 0 in an RC the capacitor already carries
.IR V / R ,
and the trapezoidal rule reads it on the very first step.
.PP
.B Step
advances the circuit by
.I h
seconds.
Each capacitor and inductor is replaced for that solve by a
.I companion
.IR model :
a conductance with a current source beside it, chosen so that the linear
solve reproduces the integration rule applied to
.I i
=
.I C
d\fIv\fP/d\fIt\fP or
.I v
=
.I L
d\fIi\fP/d\fIt\fP.
That is why a transient analysis is a sequence of linear solves and not
an ODE integration.
.PP
.B Nodev
returns the voltage at a node and
.B parti
the current through a part, both by name, from the last solve.
.B Parti
measures current from a part's first node to its second.
.PP
.B Solve
solves a small dense system by Gaussian elimination with partial
pivoting, and is exported because MNA needs one and nothing else in the
tree had one.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/circuit.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR dynsys (2),
.IR pde (2),
.IR krylov (2),
.IR circuittest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
All of these return a diagnostic string, nil on success.
A circuit that has no single answer \- a node with nothing to hold its
voltage, two voltage sources argued across each other, a loop of nothing
but voltage sources \- is refused by the solve rather than answered, and
says which unknown was not determined.
.SH BUGS
Everything here is linear and every source is constant.
There are no diodes, transistors, switches or time-varying sources, so
there is no Newton loop and no need for one; a step response is obtained
by starting from
.B begin
rather than by turning a source on.
.PP
The step size is whatever the caller passes; there is no automatic step
control and no local truncation error estimate, so a step too large for
the circuit's time constants is quietly inaccurate rather than refused.
.PP
Mutual inductance, transmission lines and small-signal AC analysis are
all absent.
